export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>The game industry has come a long way from the days of 2D side-scrolling worlds. Now games can feature huge open worlds with a myriad of details to explore. That gives the developers a challenge: they have to create realistic and functional game environments and assets with limited time and resources. Procedural generation is a common answer to this problem. However, one size cannot fit all, so we will discuss both the pros and cons of this method.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/32e1dc370ce8177ae566.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/32e1dc370ce8177ae566.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Procedural generation is not a new concept in video games. If you have ever played a loot-based RPG (Diablo for example), you will know that the drop rates and drop quality are not fixed. They are partially randomized to create a myriad of content, and in turn, increase replayability.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/3d78dfaa584b357d0428.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/3d78dfaa584b357d0428.webp\" width=\"714\" height=\"523\" style=\"--source-width:288px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/01d37b71eee76a528027.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/01d37b71eee76a528027.webp\" width=\"778\" height=\"570\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Not only did Diablo apply this method to loot drops, but also to side areas. Every dungeon you explore will differ from the previous in terms of layout and enemy encounters. Only the assets are reused.</p>\n<p>With randomized drop items and  dungeons, Diablo is able to give the player more content with limited resources.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>A well-built procedural system can ceaselessly bring new challenges and fresh experiences to the player. The maps in XCOM 2 are partially randomized which means the player has to play differently every time he enters a new one. Due to such unpredictability, they can’t learn the game by heart and has to adapt on the fly, which is integral to a strategy game like XCOM 2.</p>\n<p>Besides, procedurally generated content can adjust difficulty to fit the player’s ability to keep him engaged. For instance, the map can spawn less enemies or weaker ones when you didn’t perform well previously, and vice versa.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/9efc01b53dc25116cb1f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/9efc01b53dc25116cb1f.webp\" width=\"769\" height=\"814\" style=\"--source-width:288px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>No Man’s Sky is a game built entirely on the idea of procedural generation. In this game, you get to explore an infinite number of planets complete with landscapes, biome and animal species. However, human brain is good at noticing discernible patterns. Eventually, we will grow bored of procedurally generated content because, while they have differences, they still feel similar due to the reused assets.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/9026f6f23554e39a9d9e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/9026f6f23554e39a9d9e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"319\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/8995a922553385707851.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/8995a922553385707851.webp\" width=\"740\" height=\"395\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Another critical weakness of procedural generation is the lack of personality. Data algorithms can’t tell story by themselves. One of the reasons why linear single-player games are still favorable is the human elements. By handcrafting the content, the designers can tailor the experience with profound intentions.</p>\n<p>This Star Wars moment became iconic because it was built up with a lot of thoughts. Things like this are not possible with randomization.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>For games in general, environmental storytelling is a powerful tool to enhance the experience. In Breath of the Wild, you can find an abandoned horseback archery course next to Hateno village. There are some arrows in the house indicating of a once existed business; and the fact that it is on the main road further solidifies that.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/5112d9d4c2597c479d19.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/5112d9d4c2597c479d19.webp\" width=\"859\" height=\"478\" style=\"--source-width:288px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/ac4d3271ecbc14ca4d84.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/ac4d3271ecbc14ca4d84.webp\" width=\"838\" height=\"467\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Procedural generation can potentially result in nonsensical architecture. In Assassin’s Creed Unity, you can find a hallway on the 3<sup>rd</sup> floor of a building, a hallway with only windows and nothing else. Similar problems can occur where you have a bakery exterior with a grocery store interior.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Those problems can be avoided by building an intricate procedural generation system. You can set rules as to how the game creates new content to make sure everything fits. Far Cry 5 did this with Houdini.</p>\n<p>Natural elements are tied together and have specific reasons to exist, meaning that pure randomization doesn’t work. So, the team at Ubisoft built an ecosystem of procedural tools to make land, water and vegetation just like real life.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/e1a83814f53ae42884bb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/e1a83814f53ae42884bb.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>You still can implement environmental storytelling in a procedurally generated level. Here is the approach of an XCOM developer. Instead of asking for specific assets to craft a story, he tried to build a story around the existing assets.</p>\n<p>There is a pinky frisbee on the roof top indicating there were kids playing; the color may imply that the owner was a girl.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/ae42c1825c5f9e8b862a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/ae42c1825c5f9e8b862a.webp\" width=\"777\" height=\"433\" style=\"--source-width:288px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/85f9f30fde06aa6eb78f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/85f9f30fde06aa6eb78f.webp\" width=\"883\" height=\"466\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>This interior space is occupied by a lot of flower pots and pictures; the father’s face is cover meaning that he left them; we can tell that she rarely was rarely away from this area thanks to the coffee, remote control and the pillow.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Here is the final example. The designer wanted to show that there were men under the sewer, hiding away from the aliens. Despite living under condition, they still played chess and drank.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/eb9f4de8ec82d90d58dd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/eb9f4de8ec82d90d58dd.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"302\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>See you somewhere</strong></p>\n<p>At the end of the day, the choice to use procedural generation or not totally depends on the goal of your game. Procedural generation will come in handy when the game wants to deliver hundreds hours of content and emphasizes replayability and scale. But if your game is just a 20-hour experience with a focus on compelling narrative, handcrafting all of the content will suit better.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/f54107cf3bcffaff66ad.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/f54107cf3bcffaff66ad.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"541\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>";
